Design of `D6AuditReport.lean`:

```lean
/-
D6-weekly-release — per-declaration axiom report.
...
-/
import ReleaseCheck
import Lean.Util.CollectAxioms

open Lean Elab Command

namespace D6AuditReport

def approvedAxioms : List Name := ["propext".toName, "Classical.choice".toName, "Quot.sound".toName]
def sorryAxiom : Name := "sorryAx".toName
def nativeAxioms : List Name := ["ofReduceBool".toName, "Lean.ofReduceBool".toName]
def projectModuleRoots : List Name := ["Poincare".toName, "Probe".toName, "Ledger".toName, "Audit".toName, "ReleaseCheck".toName, "ReleaseAudit".toName, "D6AuditReport".toName]

def isProjectModule (m : Name) : Bool := projectModuleRoots.any (fun r => r.isPrefixOf m)

def projectConstants (env : Environment) : Array (Name × ConstantInfo × Name) :=
  let mods := env.header.moduleNames
  env.constants.fold (fun acc n ci =>
    match env.getModuleIdxFor? n with
    | some midx =>
        let m := mods.getD midx .anonymous
        if isProjectModule m then acc.push (n, ci, m) else acc
    | none => acc) #[]

def kindOf : ConstantInfo → String
  | .axiomInfo _ => "axiom"
  | .defnInfo v => match v.safety with | .unsafe => "unsafe_def" | .partial => "partial_def" | .safe => "def"
  | .thmInfo _ => "theorem"
  | .opaqueInfo _ => "opaque"
  | .quotInfo _ => "quot"
  | .inductInfo _ => "induct"
  | .ctorInfo _ => "ctor"
  | .recInfo _ => "rec"

end D6AuditReport

open D6AuditReport in
run_cmd do
  let env ← getEnv
  let cs := projectConstants env
  ...
  for (n, ci, m) in cs do
    let axs ← Lean.collectAxioms n
    IO.println s!"D6DECL\t{n}\t{kindOf ci}\t{m}\t{";".intercalate (axs.toList.map Name.toString)}"
  ...
  IO.println s!"D6AUDIT\tdeclarations\t{cs.size}"
  ...
```

But careful: `IO.println` in run_cmd — is IO available? `run_cmd` runs in `CommandElabM` which is `CoreM`-based with IO. Yes, `IO.println` should work since CoreM has IO. Actually in Lean 4, `CommandElabM` extends `CoreM` extends `IO`. Yes.

Sorting: `env.constants.fold` gives some order (NameMap order, not sorted). For deterministic output, collect then sort by name. Array.qsort requires Ord Name or a comparator. `Name` has `Ord`? There's `compare` on Name maybe. I can use `cs.qsort (fun a b => a.1.toString < b.1.toString)`. String comparison with `<` is fine (decidable). qsort signature: `Array.qsort (xs : Array α) (lt : α → α → Bool) : Array α`. Yes.

